what do you do if a kid is in danger  sometimes shouting at them is enough but  what if the kid is really in danger as  in just within pouncing distance of a  very hungry tiger danger you might keep  the kid quiet wait for the coast to  clear and then when it's safe  explained the danger that might seem  like the obvious answer but in nature  very few animals are capable of holding  it all in this ability to wait and  communicate information after an event  has passed or about an event that will  happen in the future  it's called displaced reference humans  do it honeybees do it and now it turns  out that orangutans do it too  researchers conducted an experiment with  seven female orangutan the Kitab a  jungle of North East Sumatra they  pretended to be predators by draping  themselves with giant sheets and  crawling along the forest floor one  sheet was covered in a tiger striped  pattern one had a pattern of abstract  colors one was white with multi-coloured  spots and the last was a plain white  sheet once the predator was spotted by  an orangutan the researcher would stop  for two minutes before moving out of  sight surprisingly the orangutans would  wait several minutes before letting out  a warning call they did this in 12 of  the 24 trials with an average delay of  seven minutes  so why the wait Sumatran orangutans have  the longest child-rearing period of any  primate Infini Wregget and stay with  their mothers for up to nine years which  allows them to develop critical force  survival skills letting out a warning  call at the first sight of a predator is  just too dangerous because it might  alert the predator to their presence but  at the same time the parent needs to  teach the infant to recognize these  situations in the future so the mothers  wait long enough that the danger has  passed but short enough that the infant  can still learn from the encounter  researchers say this clearly  demonstrates the ability to use  displaced reference and a non-human  primate something never seen before in  the wild and that suggests another way  that orangutan is one of our closest  living relatives and a prolific  communicator may be a model for how  language evolved in people who knows if  we gave a ring at hands a million years  they just might have something to say  [Music]  you  [Music]
 

